 @mycelia_ow  The RTX 2060 struggles @ high with RTX in what game then? Not every game uses the same implementation of raytracing so you're vague statement is completely incorrect. 
I never said the 2060 had faster memory than the 5600XT. 

Sure, technically you can run a raytracing benchmark with your 5700XT if you want, but you don't have any raytracing support in games. It's fine if you don't care about that though, that's perfectly valid. However, it is also perfectly valid for someone else to want to experience RTX, which they can with a 2060, even if it is the most entry level option for raytracing. No one said it's going to destroy games with RTX on @ 1440p, max but the 2060 is absolutely capable of RTX at fairly comfortable settings for the most part. Obviously it depends on the way raytracing has been implemented in to the game you're playing though.

 @juzujuzu4555  I can definitely agree the current "RTX" implementation of raytracing has a long way to go. That said, it's the only real way to currently experience it and having tried it with my 2070 Super, Im actually a lot more impressed than I thought Id be- I seriously thought I wouldn't give a shit but it made me excited for the future of the technology.

I can definitely see AMD being more likely to properly bring raytracing to the mainstream at a good value but it'll take them some time.

I can't justify the 'NVidia tax' to you though. I definitely don't disagree with you there and am very unhappy with the price hikes this generation. Anything higher end than the 2070 Super just doesn't even seem worth it, and even then the 2070 Super is already a pretty tough sell. Only reason I got mine was because I didn't want to deal with any driver headaches but wanted around 1080Ti performance. 2060 Super wasn't gonna cut it for me and I just wasn't comfortable buying in to Navi at launch.

 @adahepper2665  Yeah, I mean I definitely wouldn't enable RTX in a competitive game. It is still pretty nice to experience IMO, and luckily we have the choice to enable/disable at out leisure so we don't need to suffer a performance loss in competitive games. 


With my 2070 Super, I can run SotTR @ high+ settings with RTX on high and maintain well above 60FPS @ 1440p, that's much better performance than I was expecting and the RTX implementation in SotTR is far more noticeable and impressive than I ever expected it would be. 


I can't speak for Quake RTX as I never tried that one, it may well be a piss poor implementation of raytracing and RTX. However, if I can play comfortably in SotTR at 1440p with my 2070S, an 2060 is definitely capable of that at 1080p, for $300 that's not nothing. I'm definitely not arguing it's a great implementation of RTX currently but it's still something, it IS still a feature the AMD GPU's don't have and to some people, that's enough. Just because you don't think it's worth it, doesn't mean nobody does- you get me?

 @ArcDragoon  Yeah, Windows updates often break one-off and proprietary support software and sometimes screw with drivers. It was only recently that major updates stopped breaking the CPU fan control on my laptop; fixing that required reinstalling some software from the manufacturer, which has a permanent home on my drive as a result. And that was just the fan control; other things like color profiles had massive bugs involving games that were brought on by updates too, but thankfully they fixed that.


Windows also has a tendency to be less stable on older or uncommon hardware in my experience; my friend had an issue where every time Windows installed an update, all of his settings would be reverted. That included firewall settings, which meant in order to use Hamachi he frequently had to manually edit its firewall properties.


Lastly, as an anecdote, my brother once unknowingly installed Windows while a bad stick of RAM was in use, and he had to wipe the drive because, evidently, his Windows copy was corrupted.

